If you recurse on both the subterms and the definitions at the same time, this will easily stack overflow for any program that isn't super small.
I recommend recursion only in the subterms since we know users will usually write definitions of reasonable size.

You can implement this by returning the newly found definitions here with `Term::find_used_definition(...) -> HashSet<DefId>` and then in `DefinitionBook::prune()` you can `find_used_definitions` on the new set of found definitions. Something like:
```
let mut to_visit = HashSet::new();
while let Some(def_id) = to_visit.pop() {
  let body = &self.defs[def_id].body;
  let new_defs = body.find_used_definitions(used, &self.defs);
  used.extend(new_defs.iter().copied());
  to_visit.extend(new_defs.into_iter());
}
```

