The walk is one object: `seen` numbers instances in opening order, `on` maps each family on the path to its value arguments' shortest keys, and `low` carries the earliest instance met back up (see the review body).

```suggestion
// on them holds). on keeps each family's shortest value keys on the path;
// an answer is kept per file (p by its text: it captures nothing) if it
// holds or met only instances opened under it: a new walk agrees.
function ty_holds(A: HTerm | null, p: Name | ((t: HTerm | null) => boolean),
  w = { seen: new Map<string, number>(), on: new Map<Name, number[]>(),
    low: Infinity }): boolean {
  const t = ty_wnf(A);
  if (t?.$ === "Lam") {
    return ty_holds(t.f(DUMMY), p, w);
```

`re` reads the shortest keys in O(arguments) instead of scanning and copying the path. An instance's answer is kept in `FL.hold` when it holds, or when its fields met only instances opened under it.

```suggestion
  if (t.x.some((x, i) => !vs[i] && ty_holds(x, p, w))) {
    return true;
  }
  const xs = t.x.map((x, i) => vs[i] ? Bend.term_key(Bend.term_lower(x)) : "");
  const ys = w.on.get(t.k);
  const re = !!ys && xs.some((x, i) => x.length > ys[i]);
  const key = t.k + (!re && xs);
  const at = w.seen.get(key);
  if (tld?.$ !== "ADT" || at !== undefined) {
    w.low = Math.min(w.low, at ?? Infinity);
    return false;
  }
  const id = JSON.stringify([String(p), key, ...w.on]);
  if (FL.hold.has(id)) {
    return FL.hold.get(id)!;
  }
  const [n, low] = [w.seen.size, w.low];
  w.seen.set(key, n);
  w.low = Infinity;
  const us = t.x.map((x, i) => re && vs[i] ? DUMMY : x);
  w.on.set(t.k, xs.map((x, i) => Math.min(x.length, ys?.[i] ?? Infinity)));
  const r = tld.c.some((c) => ctr_doms(c, us).some((f) =>
    ty_holds(f, p, w)));
  ys ? w.on.set(t.k, ys) : w.on.delete(t.k);
  if (r || w.low >= n) {
    FL.hold.set(id, r);
  }
  w.low = Math.min(low, w.low);
  return r;
```

Making `-Wtentative-definition-compat` an error in the dead copy also rejects valid HID code: a static global declared first and defined later.

```c
static const struct N HID(a);
static const struct N HID(b) = {&HID(a)};
static const struct N HID(a) = {&HID(b)};
```

Copy 1 compiles. In copy 2, Homebrew clang 22 reports `error: duplicate declaration of 'HID2_..._a' is invalid in C++`, so a correct package can't be built natively.

Separately, this pragma isn't guarded. Clang before 21, including Apple clang 17 (the macOS default `clang`, which `cc_find` picks first), prints `warning: unknown warning group '-Wtentative-definition-compat', ignored` once per HID module on every `-o` build. The check then does nothing. Wrapping it in `#if __has_warning("-Wtentative-definition-compat")` fixes that.
